  const biography = "Jessie Irene Aurin was born on 25 October 1879 in Crystal Township, Oceana County, Michigan. Her childhood began in a Lake Michigan county where sawmills, farms and young villages were growing together. Crystal Township had been organized in 1865, only fourteen years before her birth. By 1880, while she was still an infant, Jessie was living in neighboring Elbridge Township.\n\nThe countryside belonged to a much older Great Lakes history. Odawa and other Anishinaabe peoples had longstanding ties to the region, and Native communities continued in Crystal and Elbridge as treaties and settlement changed control of the land. Around them, roads and bridges connected scattered farms with villages, mills and the lakeshore. Timber brought work and trade; cleared land supported agriculture and, increasingly, orchards.\n\nLake Michigan connected these rural communities to distant markets. Ships carried lumber and farm goods toward Chicago and other growing Midwestern cities. Jessie grew up during the transition from a landscape dominated by forests and sawmills to one increasingly shaped by fields, fruit growing and local commerce. Her early homes were close together within Oceana County, but the family she later raised would move farther into northern Michigan.\n\nOn 5 November 1899, twenty-year-old Jessie married Richard Francis Skinner in Elbridge Township. Richard was thirty-six, a Michigan-born farmer residing in nearby Leavitt Township. It was Jessie’s first marriage and Richard’s second. Their marriage license had been issued two days before the wedding.\n\nJessie entered a household that included Richard’s older children, Millie Irene and Marcie Leo. Marcie was a boy, born in 1894. The 1900 household brought Jessie, Richard and the two children together in Leavitt Township, where farming remained central to Richard’s working life.\n\nJessie and Richard’s children were Clifford D., Marian Beulah, Ellen Madelia, John Andrew, Richard Francis and Amanda Melvina. Marian Beulah later married Charles Albert Brenay and became the mother of Marion Beulah Brenay. Through Marian, Jessie’s family joined the Brenay line and the generations that followed.\n\nBy 1920 Jessie and Richard were living with several children in Briley Township, Montmorency County. This northern inland setting had been shaped by white-pine and hardwood logging, rail connections and rivers carrying timber toward mills and Alpena. As the great lumber boom receded, farming and other rural livelihoods took a larger place in local life. The family’s move carried Jessie from Michigan’s western lakeshore county into a different part of the state’s forest and agricultural country.\n\nBy April 1930, Jessie was living in Mesa, Arizona, in the household of her daughter Marian and son-in-law Charles Brenay. Her youngest daughter, eleven-year-old Amanda, was there too. The census recorded Jessie, then fifty, as Charles’s mother-in-law. This chapter carried her family connections far beyond the Michigan communities of her earlier life.\n\nJessie’s life ended in Spratt, a farming community in Alpena County. Local farms raised cattle, hay, grains and potatoes; schools, stores, mail service and churches drew neighboring households together. The surrounding area also developed a berry-growing trade, with produce shipped by refrigerated rail. These connections linked a small rural community to markets beyond northeastern Michigan.\n\nJessie died on 5 March 1956 at seventy-six and was buried on 8 March in Spratt Cemetery near Lachine. Her life connected the young Oceana townships of her birth with northern Michigan’s rural communities and family life in Arizona, and her children carried those family ties into another generation.";
